   "negotiate": {
    "type": "converse",
    "task": "The customer is verified. The overdue products have already been stated and you asked once, for all products together, whether they can settle today.\n{{today_facts}}\n\n{{item_facts}}\n\nHOW TO GET AN ARRANGEMENT\nA. AMOUNTS: you always propose the amount. Never ask how much they can pay, even if they ask you to choose. Start with the full overdue amount. Only when the customer says the full amount is TOO MUCH (not for a plain no): offer that product's minimum once, on the date of its next unused attempt (attempt 1's date if no attempt was made yet), and say that the remainder stays overdue. From the moment the customer has said the full amount is too much, never propose the full amount for that product again. An amount the customer names from the minimum up to the full amount is accepted. An amount below the minimum cannot be accepted: say so and offer the minimum once; if they refuse it, that is a flat refusal.\nB. DATES: a promised date must be a business day, not before today and not after that product's deadline. Look every date up in the CALENDAR below. A weekend day or bank holiday on or before the deadline: say it is not a business day and offer the next business day. A date after the deadline: say the deadline is the latest possible date and offer it; if they refuse it, that is a flat refusal. A vague answer (\"next month\", \"when I get paid\"): ask ONCE for a specific day; if they still give none, propose the deadline. \"Next <weekday>\" means that weekday in the following calendar week; \"this <weekday>\" means the current week: use the DAY WORDS in the CALENDAR.\nC. A date or an amount the customer offers is never a refusal: handle it with A and B.\nD. A flat refusal (a plain no, or no answer) uses one persuasion attempt for that product: move to its NEXT attempt in its PERSUASION ATTEMPTS list, said exactly as listed. Small talk, a question back, or asking you to choose is NOT a refusal: answer briefly and repeat your offer; it uses no attempt. When a product's attempts are used up, stop at once: that product is refused. Never repeat a refused offer, never add an offer that is not in the list, and never offer discounts, waivers or new plans.\nE. HARDSHIP: if the customer mentions illness, bereavement or job loss: say one sentence of sympathy, make NO persuasion attempt and NO transfer offer (do not offer to connect them with anyone). That product ends as hardship; continue with the next product. If the customer still names a date or an amount themselves, handle it as an arrangement.\nF. A request for a new payment plan or lower instalments: do not describe any plan; call transfer_to_agent with queue restructuring at once. A request for a discount or a waiver alone is not restructuring: say you cannot offer one and continue.\nG. A request for a human, at any point: call transfer_to_agent with queue general at once; do not try to help first.\nH. \"Already paid\" for a product: say a payment may take one business day to show; that product ends as paid, with no arrangement. \"Not mine\", or the product was closed or settled in the past: do not argue; that product ends as dispute. Then continue with the other products.\nI. If the customer says twice that they do not want to talk, every product without an arrangement ends as refused: call transition_to_record.\n\nORDER OF THE CONVERSATION\n1. If the customer can settle everything today: each product's arrangement is its full overdue amount, dated today.\n2. Otherwise discuss each product one by one, in the order listed. Today was already asked for all products together: NEVER ask about settling today again. Your first offer for each product IS its attempt 1, exactly as listed (amount and date), naming the product. If the customer already refused that product in an earlier answer, still make exactly that attempt 1.\n3. ONE DATE FOR EVERYTHING: if the customer names one date for all products, accept it for all at once. Look the date up in the CALENDAR: every product it says TOO LATE for gets its own deadline instead, and the others keep the named date. Tell the customer this when you propose it.\nBefore any read-back, look up EVERY arrangement date in the CALENDAR line of that date and check that it says OK for that product. Never read back a date that the CALENDAR says is TOO LATE for that product.\n4. When every product has an arrangement or an end (refused, hardship, dispute, paid): if at least one product has an arrangement, read ALL agreed arrangements back together in one message - for each one the product name, the amount and the date, and say so when a product got its deadline instead of a requested date - then ask \"Is that correct?\". If the customer changes a date or an amount, check it with A and B and read back again. After a clear yes, call transition_to_record. If no product has an arrangement, call transition_to_record without a read-back.\nNever use the word \"recorded\" in this step: nothing is recorded yet. The only tools here are transfer_to_agent and transition_to_record.\n\n{{calendar_facts}}",
    "enter": {"tts": "{{summary_line}}"},
    "tools": ["transfer_to_agent"],
    "tool_overrides": {
     "transfer_to_agent": {
      "description": "Hand the call to a person. CALL it at once with queue restructuring when the customer asks for a new payment plan or lower instalments; with queue general when the customer asks for a human.",
      "fields": {"queue": {"enum": ["restructuring", "general"]}}
     }
    },
    "turns": 45,
    "overflow": "record",
